1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:01,680
Léa: Hello everyone!

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:03,600
Welcome to The Cozy French,

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,720
your little get-together for learning French

4
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:10,320
with natural, warm conversations.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,200
I'm Léa,

6
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,320
and I'm very happy to be with you again today.

7
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:15,760
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu!

8
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:17,390
Hello, dear friends!

9
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:18,720
Whether you're at home,

10
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:22,040
on public transport or making a coffee,

11
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:23,920
get comfortable.

12
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,880
We're delighted to spend this moment with you.

13
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:29,140
Léa: Mathieu, I have a question.

14
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:31,790
Do you feel motivated today?

15
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:34,830
Mathieu: Motivated? I'd say...

16
00:00:34,830 --> 00:00:38,360
fifty percent. Maybe forty.

17
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:42,600
Léa: Your motivation just dropped in five seconds!

18
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,800
Mathieu: That's exactly my problem!

19
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,560
On Sunday evening, I was full of energy.

20
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,720
I'd made a wonderful list: do sport,

21
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:53,320
read more,

22
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:56,800
cook healthily and tidy my flat.

23
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,500
Léa: All that at the same time?

24
00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:02,720
Mathieu: Yes! But on Monday morning, looking at my list,

25
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,540
I wondered who had

26
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:07,310
come up with such a complicated plan.

27
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:09,830
Léa: But it was you, Mathieu!

28
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:12,750
Mathieu: Yes, but Sunday-evening Mathieu

29
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:16,280
is much more ambitious than Monday-morning Mathieu.

30
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:17,470
Léa: I think lots

31
00:01:17,470 --> 00:01:20,320
of our listeners know that feeling.

32
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:22,920
You start out with lots of enthusiasm,

33
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:24,600
then a few days later,

34
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:27,700
you feel tired or discouraged.

35
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,280
Mathieu: Exactly. And today,

36
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:32,640
we're going to talk about this question:

37
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:35,990
how do you keep going when you feel like giving up?

38
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,910
Léa: Our topic is "Don't Give Up Yet",

39
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:42,150
in other words "N'abandonne pas maintenant".

40
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,600
Maybe you're learning French,

41
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:46,600
working on a project

42
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,000
or trying to build new habits.

43
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,080
Mathieu: And maybe right now,

44
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:55,100
you feel like you're not making progress anymore.

45
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:58,480
If that's you, this conversation is for you.

46
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,030
Léa: But careful,

47
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:02,840
we're not going to tell you to work non-stop.

48
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,390
Resting,

49
00:02:04,390 --> 00:02:06,960
slowing down and knowing your limits

50
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:09,680
are important things too.

51
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:11,000
Mathieu: And as always,

52
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,380
we'll discover a few French

53
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:16,360
expressions to talk about motivation,

54
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,880
difficulties and progress.

55
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:22,020
Léa: Before we start, tell us in the comments:

56
00:02:22,070 --> 00:02:24,870
what's one thing you'd like to keep doing,

57
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:27,150
even if it isn't always easy?

58
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,080
Mathieu: One short sentence in French is enough.

59
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,240
And if you like our conversations,

60
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,400
remember to subscribe to The Cozy French.

61
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:37,550
Now, let's begin!

62
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,280
Léa: So, Mathieu, back to your list.

63
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:43,750
Why were you so motivated on Sunday?

64
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:47,020
Mathieu: Because I was already picturing the result.

65
00:02:47,070 --> 00:02:49,030
I saw myself in great shape,

66
00:02:49,030 --> 00:02:52,030
with a perfectly tidy flat

67
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:54,800
and a much better organised life.

68
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,200
Léa: So you mostly imagined the final result,

69
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:00,400
but not the daily effort.

70
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,680
Mathieu: Exactly. In my imagination,

71
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,880
I was never tired.

72
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:07,980
I never had any problems

73
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:11,200
and I had all the time I needed.

74
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:12,750
Léa: That's why our plans

75
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:15,220
sometimes become hard to stick to.

76
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:18,800
We design them for an ideal version of ourselves,

77
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:21,000
not for our real life.

78
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,800
Mathieu: True. On Monday, I want to do everything.

79
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:25,720
And by Thursday,

80
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:27,460
I already struggle to find

81
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:30,400
the energy for a short walk.

82
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:34,200
Léa: But that doesn't mean your goal is bad.

83
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,000
Motivation naturally goes up and down.

84
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,270
Some days, we have lots of energy.

85
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:42,620
Other days, much less.

86
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:46,890
Mathieu: Still, when I miss a session, I often tell myself:

87
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:49,370
"I'm really lazy.

88
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:51,350
I never finish anything."

89
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:54,200
Léa: And that's exactly a habit to change.

90
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,880
You missed one session,

91
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:59,400
but that doesn't define your whole personality.

92
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:00,950
Mathieu: So I could say:

93
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:03,210
"Today, I was tired

94
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:05,550
and I didn't do my session."

95
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,470
Léa: Exactly. You describe the situation

96
00:04:08,470 --> 00:04:11,020
without judging yourself too harshly.

97
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:14,000
Mathieu: It's true that changes a lot of things.

98
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,870
One difficult day doesn't mean

99
00:04:16,870 --> 00:04:19,400
my whole project is a failure.

100
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:21,450
Léa: Absolutely. And sometimes,

101
00:04:21,450 --> 00:04:24,400
instead of looking for more motivation,

102
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:26,530
we should simply make

103
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:28,800
our plan more realistic.

104
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,720
Mathieu: Like replacing an hour of sport

105
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,550
with fifteen minutes of walking?

106
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:37,020
Léa: Yes. Or reading five pages rather than thirty.

107
00:04:37,070 --> 00:04:39,380
What matters is creating habits

108
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:42,800
that can fit into our daily life.

109
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:45,400
Mathieu: You know, I like that idea.

110
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:47,120
Because a simple plan

111
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,600
that you can follow for several months

112
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,400
is probably more useful

113
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:56,200
than an extraordinary plan you drop after three days.

114
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:58,970
Léa: Exactly. In French,

115
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:03,220
we can talk about "une habitude durable".

116
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:05,320
"Durable" means that something

117
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:08,000
can go on for a long time.

118
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,400
Mathieu: For example:

119
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,940
"I prefer a simple

120
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:15,200
and lasting routine to an overly demanding plan."

121
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:15,990
Léa: Very good.

122
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:20,200
And that works for learning French too.

123
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:22,480
Mathieu: Imagine someone who decides

124
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:25,800
to study for two hours every evening.

125
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:28,820
At first, they're very enthusiastic.

126
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:31,420
Léa: Then they get home late from work,

127
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:33,240
they have commitments

128
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:36,000
and they start missing some sessions.

129
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,400
Mathieu: So they tell themselves they lack discipline.

130
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:42,680
Léa: And yet, maybe two hours every evening

131
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:46,000
simply doesn't fit their life.

132
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,800
They could try twenty minutes on busy days

133
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:52,800
and a longer session at the weekend.

134
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:54,870
Mathieu: It would be less impressive,

135
00:05:54,870 --> 00:05:57,020
but easier to keep up.

136
00:05:57,070 --> 00:05:58,060
Léa: Exactly.

137
00:05:58,110 --> 00:06:02,600
The best plan isn't always the most ambitious one.

138
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:06,800
It's the one that helps us move forward without wearing ourselves out.

139
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:10,200
Mathieu: You know, that reminds me of something.

140
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:11,590
Last year,

141
00:06:11,590 --> 00:06:14,600
I decided to read a novel in French.

142
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:17,150
Léa: Oh really? And how did it go?

143
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:19,170
Mathieu: Very badly at first!

144
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,600
I'd chosen a book that was far too difficult.

145
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:24,150
I had to look up almost

146
00:06:24,150 --> 00:06:26,220
every word in the dictionary.

147
00:06:26,270 --> 00:06:26,970
Léa: Oh dear!

148
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:29,350
That must have been exhausting.

149
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:31,070
Mathieu: After twenty minutes,

150
00:06:31,070 --> 00:06:34,000
I didn't even understand the story anymore.

151
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,980
So I gave up on the book and thought

152
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:40,000
my French wasn't good enough.

153
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,090
Léa: But maybe the problem

154
00:06:42,090 --> 00:06:44,800
was simply the choice of book.

155
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:48,060
Mathieu: Exactly. A few weeks later,

156
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:50,600
I tried shorter stories,

157
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:53,420
with more accessible vocabulary.

158
00:06:53,470 --> 00:06:57,200
And this time, I rediscovered the joy of reading.

159
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:01,200
Léa: So you didn't need to give up your goal.

160
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:04,800
You simply needed to change your method.

161
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:06,350
Mathieu: Yes. And I think

162
00:07:06,350 --> 00:07:09,400
we often forget that option.

163
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:11,430
Léa: When something isn't working,

164
00:07:11,430 --> 00:07:13,000
we sometimes feel

165
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
that only two choices exist:

166
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,220
carry on exactly the same way or stop everything.

167
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:24,200
Mathieu: Whereas there's a third option:

168
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:26,000
trying a different way.

169
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,600
Léa: Exactly. You can adapt your method,

170
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:34,400
ask for advice or temporarily scale down your goals.

171
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:35,740
Mathieu: But sometimes, Léa,

172
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:39,800
I think you're simply too tired to keep going.

173
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:40,800
Léa: That's true.

174
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:44,400
And in that case, a break may be necessary.

175
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:47,200
Our energy isn't unlimited.

176
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:48,310
Mathieu: So taking a break

177
00:07:48,310 --> 00:07:51,020
doesn't necessarily mean giving up?

178
00:07:51,070 --> 00:07:55,000
Léa: Absolutely not. A break can help you recover,

179
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,800
think things over and come back with a fresh perspective.

180
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:03,400
Mathieu: I really like the expression "avoir besoin de souffler".

181
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,950
Léa: Yes. In this context, "souffler"

182
00:08:05,950 --> 00:08:08,000
means taking a moment

183
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,600
to rest after a difficult period.

184
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:14,520
Mathieu: For example: "After this busy week,

185
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:17,420
I really need a little breather."

186
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:20,400
Léa: Exactly. And we shouldn't feel

187
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:23,600
guilty every time we take a rest.

188
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:25,630
Mathieu: You know, for a long time,

189
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:28,170
I thought a disciplined person

190
00:08:28,170 --> 00:08:31,000
always had to move forward at the same pace.

191
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:32,250
Léa: And today?

192
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:34,520
Mathieu: Today, I think you can

193
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:37,400
slow down without giving up on your goal.

194
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:39,600
Léa: That's a nice distinction.

195
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:43,420
Keeping going doesn't necessarily mean doing more.

196
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:46,450
Sometimes, it simply means finding

197
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:49,000
a gentler way to move forward.

198
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,790
Mathieu: And sometimes, what holds us back isn't tiredness,

199
00:08:52,790 --> 00:08:55,000
but a big disappointment.

200
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,200
Léa: Yes. I know a story that illustrates this really well.

201
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,400
Mathieu: I'm listening.

202
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:04,200
Léa: A friend of mine dreamed of becoming a pastry chef.

203
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:06,400
She loved making desserts

204
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:10,620
and spent a lot of time learning new techniques.

205
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:11,960
Mathieu: Ah, pastry!

206
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:14,800
Now that's a subject I fully approve of.

207
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:17,650
Léa: I knew you'd say that!

208
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:19,300
But listen to what happened next.

209
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:21,490
After several months of training,

210
00:09:21,490 --> 00:09:23,500
she entered a competition.

211
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:26,200
Mathieu: She must have been really excited.

212
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:27,200
Léa: Yes.

213
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:31,800
She'd worked for weeks on a special dessert.

214
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,760
But on the day of the competition,

215
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:37,200
part of her creation got damaged.

216
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:40,220
Mathieu: Oh no! What a disappointment.

217
00:09:40,270 --> 00:09:42,130
Léa: She was so sad

218
00:09:42,130 --> 00:09:45,400
that she started to doubt her abilities.

219
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,020
She kept telling herself:

220
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:49,550
"Maybe I'm not cut out for this job."

221
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:50,580
Mathieu: That's hard.

222
00:09:50,580 --> 00:09:52,890
Because she'd put a lot

223
00:09:52,890 --> 00:09:55,000
of time and energy into her dream.

224
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,500
Léa: Exactly. But what struck me

225
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:00,350
was that she no longer just said

226
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:02,350
"My dessert didn't work out".

227
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:06,120
She said "I'm not capable of succeeding".

228
00:10:06,170 --> 00:10:09,170
Mathieu: Ah... She was turning a difficult event

229
00:10:09,170 --> 00:10:12,500
into a judgement about herself.

230
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:15,900
Léa: Yes. And that often happens after a failure.

231
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:18,560
We forget that the result of a single

232
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:21,900
experience doesn't sum up all our abilities.

233
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:23,650
Mathieu: So, what did she do?

234
00:10:23,700 --> 00:10:26,000
Léa: She took a few weeks off.

235
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,270
Then she talked with a former teacher,

236
00:10:29,270 --> 00:10:30,730
who helped her understand

237
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:33,400
her mistakes and regain her confidence.

238
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:35,020
Mathieu: And she started again?

239
00:10:35,070 --> 00:10:37,300
Léa: Yes, but gently.

240
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:39,360
She started making desserts

241
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:40,800
for her family and friends,

242
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:43,800
just to rediscover the joy of creating.

243
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:45,050
Mathieu: And today?

244
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:47,100
Léa: She still works in that field.

245
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:50,500
Her path wasn't exactly the one she imagined,

246
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:51,700
but she learned

247
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:55,800
not to let one disappointment decide her future.

248
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,500
Mathieu: I like that story.

249
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:00,160
It reminds me that sometimes you have to tell the difference between

250
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:04,420
the fear of starting again and a real wish to give up.

251
00:11:04,470 --> 00:11:08,300
Léa: Exactly. My friend still loved pastry.

252
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:11,600
She was simply afraid of going through the same disappointment.

253
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:13,950
Mathieu: And that fear is understandable.

254
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Léa: Of course.

255
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,220
We shouldn't always try to turn

256
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:18,940
a painful experience immediately

257
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:21,700
into a positive message.

258
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:25,400
You can be sad, disappointed and need time.

259
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:25,970
Mathieu: You know,

260
00:11:25,970 --> 00:11:28,270
there's a French expression

261
00:11:28,270 --> 00:11:30,900
that fits this situation well:

262
00:11:30,900 --> 00:11:32,920
"encaisser un coup dur".

263
00:11:32,970 --> 00:11:35,700
Léa: Spot on. "Encaisser un coup dur"

264
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:39,700
means going through a particularly difficult experience.

265
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:43,400
Mathieu: For example: "After failing his exam,

266
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:46,600
he needed time to take the blow."

267
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:50,200
Léa: Exactly. And after stepping back,

268
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:52,100
you can think about what comes next.

269
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:56,100
Mathieu: But if you've already tried several times without success,

270
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:57,550
how do you stay confident?

271
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,630
Léa: Maybe by no longer looking

272
00:11:59,630 --> 00:12:01,620
only at the final result.

273
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:03,150
It can help to notice

274
00:12:03,150 --> 00:12:05,700
what we've learned along the way.

275
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:09,200
Mathieu: So even if I haven't reached my goal yet,

276
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:12,500
some progress can already be worth something.

277
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:14,770
Léa: Absolutely. And that's especially

278
00:12:14,770 --> 00:12:16,900
true when learning a language.

279
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:18,100
Mathieu: Ah yes.

280
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:19,360
Because you can study

281
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:21,200
French for several months

282
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:24,600
and feel like you keep making the same mistakes.

283
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:27,450
Léa: And yet, if we compare our current level

284
00:12:27,450 --> 00:12:29,220
with where we were six months ago,

285
00:12:29,270 --> 00:12:32,000
we sometimes notice big differences.

286
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:33,600
Mathieu: For example, before,

287
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:36,800
you only understood a few words in a podcast.

288
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:37,800
Léa: And today,

289
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:41,100
maybe you can follow a whole conversation,

290
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:44,200
even if some expressions are still difficult.

291
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:47,200
Mathieu: Or before, you were afraid to speak,

292
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:50,400
whereas today, you dare to ask questions.

293
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:52,650
Léa: Exactly. And sometimes,

294
00:12:52,650 --> 00:12:55,900
progress isn't about making no more mistakes.

295
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:59,020
It's about managing to keep going despite them.

296
00:12:59,070 --> 00:13:00,800
Mathieu: I really like that idea.

297
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:02,760
Because confidence isn't measured

298
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:05,700
only by how many words you know.

299
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:07,930
Léa: Yes. It also grows when

300
00:13:07,930 --> 00:13:10,300
we discover that we can communicate,

301
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:11,900
even imperfectly.

302
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:13,930
Mathieu: Like when you forget a word,

303
00:13:13,930 --> 00:13:17,100
but find another way to explain your idea.

304
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:20,300
Léa: Exactly. Instead of stopping completely,

305
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:21,500
you rephrase.

306
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:23,300
Mathieu: And after the conversation,

307
00:13:23,300 --> 00:13:27,020
you realise you managed to make yourself understood.

308
00:13:27,070 --> 00:13:29,900
Léa: That's a small win worth noticing.

309
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:32,600
Mathieu: You know, we talk a lot about progress,

310
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:35,300
but sometimes, my problem is different.

311
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:38,500
I simply don't know where to start.

312
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:39,550
Léa: Why?

313
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:42,500
Mathieu: Because I have too many things to do.

314
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,100
I make a list of fifteen

315
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:48,400
tasks and they all seem important.

316
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:49,650
Léa: And then?

317
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:52,200
Mathieu: Then I feel overwhelmed.

318
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:54,300
I start by tidying my desk

319
00:13:54,300 --> 00:13:56,920
or answering little messages,

320
00:13:56,970 --> 00:14:00,000
but I put off the main task.

321
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,030
Léa: So you're busy,

322
00:14:02,030 --> 00:14:03,760
but you're not really making progress

323
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:05,400
on what matters most.

324
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:10,000
Mathieu: Exactly. And in the evening, I feel discouraged.

325
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,900
Léa: Maybe you don't need more motivation.

326
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:18,000
Maybe you simply need to choose one priority.

327
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,150
Mathieu: Just one?

328
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:21,550
Léa: Yes. Ask yourself:

329
00:14:21,550 --> 00:14:22,880
"If I could finish

330
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:25,420
only one important thing today,

331
00:14:25,470 --> 00:14:27,150
which one would I choose?"

332
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:29,240
Mathieu: That's a good question.

333
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:31,320
But it forces me to accept

334
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:33,200
that I can't do everything.

335
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:35,480
Léa: Exactly. And sometimes,

336
00:14:35,480 --> 00:14:38,300
that acceptance can be a relief.

337
00:14:38,300 --> 00:14:41,000
Mathieu: Because instead of looking at fifteen tasks,

338
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,500
I can focus my attention on just one.

339
00:14:44,500 --> 00:14:47,530
Léa: That's it. And once that task is started,

340
00:14:47,530 --> 00:14:50,600
the rest can seem a little less hard.

341
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:54,320
Mathieu: In French, we say "se sentir débordé"

342
00:14:54,370 --> 00:14:56,950
when you have too much to handle, right?

343
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,640
Léa: Exactly. For example:

344
00:14:59,640 --> 00:15:00,500
"Right now,

345
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:02,200
I'm swamped at work

346
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:04,300
and I'm struggling to get organised."

347
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:05,600
Mathieu: And "prioriser"

348
00:15:05,650 --> 00:15:09,200
means choosing what has to come first.

349
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:10,320
Léa: Very good.

350
00:15:10,370 --> 00:15:12,040
Prioritising doesn't mean

351
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:15,200
the other tasks don't matter at all.

352
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:17,760
It simply means we have to

353
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,520
make choices sometimes.

354
00:15:19,570 --> 00:15:20,160
Mathieu: You know,

355
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:22,840
I think my Sunday-evening plan

356
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:26,700
mostly needed a good dose of realism.

357
00:15:26,700 --> 00:15:29,200
Léa: I completely agree!

358
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:32,800
Mathieu: But I have another bad habit too.

359
00:15:32,800 --> 00:15:34,400
When I miss a day,

360
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:37,400
I want to start everything over from scratch.

361
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:38,550
Léa: What do you mean?

362
00:15:38,600 --> 00:15:41,600
Mathieu: If I miss my sports session on Tuesday,

363
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:45,750
I tell myself: "Oh well, I'll start again next Monday."

364
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:48,220
Léa: But why wait for Monday?

365
00:15:48,270 --> 00:15:49,500
Mathieu: I don't know!

366
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:52,400
Monday feels kind of official.

367
00:15:52,400 --> 00:15:56,000
It's as if everything could start again cleanly.

368
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,400
Léa: And yet, one missed day doesn't erase all your past efforts.

369
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:01,300
Mathieu: True.

370
00:16:01,300 --> 00:16:02,600
So now,

371
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:05,900
I simply try to pick it up again the next day.

372
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:07,910
Léa: That's it. You don't need

373
00:16:07,910 --> 00:16:12,000
to wait for a new week or a new year to start again.

374
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:15,900
Mathieu: You can "pick up where you left off".

375
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:19,960
Léa: Exactly. This expression means starting

376
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:22,450
an activity again after a break,

377
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:25,300
without necessarily going back to square one.

378
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:26,620
Mathieu: For example:

379
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:29,400
"I stopped French for a few weeks,

380
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:33,100
but I'm going to pick up where I left off."

381
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:36,600
Léa: Very good. And that's important for our listeners.

382
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,690
Maybe some of you have

383
00:16:38,690 --> 00:16:42,020
interrupted your learning for several months.

384
00:16:42,070 --> 00:16:44,130
Mathieu: You had a lot of work,

385
00:16:44,130 --> 00:16:46,320
family responsibilities

386
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:48,500
or simply needed a rest.

387
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:51,300
Léa: But you can come back calmly.

388
00:16:51,300 --> 00:16:52,490
You don't need

389
00:16:52,490 --> 00:16:55,500
to keep blaming yourself for that break.

390
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:59,000
Mathieu: And even if you've forgotten some things,

391
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,100
that doesn't mean everything has disappeared.

392
00:17:02,100 --> 00:17:06,400
Léa: Exactly. You can review what you need,

393
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:10,420
find your rhythm again and keep going step by step.

394
00:17:10,470 --> 00:17:12,900
Mathieu: You know, I really like this idea:

395
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:15,290
you can come back without turning

396
00:17:15,290 --> 00:17:18,600
your return into a huge event.

397
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:23,200
Léa: Yes. Sometimes, all it takes is reopening your book,

398
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:25,400
listening to a conversation again

399
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:28,000
or picking up a small habit again.

400
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,750
Mathieu: And what if you still don't feel very motivated?

401
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:33,500
Léa: You can start with something simple,

402
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:35,430
as long as your energy

403
00:17:35,430 --> 00:17:37,920
and your situation allow it.

404
00:17:37,970 --> 00:17:40,780
Mathieu: Like listening to ten minutes of French

405
00:17:40,780 --> 00:17:43,600
instead of planning a big session.

406
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:47,300
Léa: Exactly. But if you're truly exhausted,

407
00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:50,000
you can also choose to rest.

408
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,400
Mathieu: So there aren't just two options:

409
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:56,900
do everything perfectly or give up on everything.

410
00:17:56,900 --> 00:17:58,760
Léa: That's it. There are lots

411
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:01,800
of options between those two extremes.

412
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:02,700
Mathieu: You know, Léa,

413
00:18:02,700 --> 00:18:06,400
I'd like to ask you a slightly harder question.

414
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:07,820
Léa: I'm listening.

415
00:18:07,870 --> 00:18:10,950
Mathieu: Should you always persevere?

416
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,300
Because sometimes,

417
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:14,500
we keep going simply because we're afraid

418
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:17,400
to admit a project isn't right for us anymore.

419
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:19,600
Léa: That's an important question.

420
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:20,110
And no,

421
00:18:20,110 --> 00:18:23,200
you shouldn't keep going at any cost.

422
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:27,200
Mathieu: So our title "Don't give up now"

423
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:29,900
doesn't mean "Never give up".

424
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:32,150
Léa: Exactly. If a goal

425
00:18:32,150 --> 00:18:34,320
no longer meets our needs,

426
00:18:34,370 --> 00:18:36,350
if a situation is hurting us

427
00:18:36,350 --> 00:18:38,600
or if our circumstances have changed,

428
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:42,400
it can be sensible to take another direction.

429
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:45,600
Mathieu: But how do you know if you really want to stop

430
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:49,150
or if you're just going through a hard time?

431
00:18:49,200 --> 00:18:52,800
Léa: You can step back and ask yourself a few questions.

432
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:55,950
Does this goal still matter to me?

433
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,250
Am I simply tired?

434
00:18:58,300 --> 00:19:00,350
Is my method working?

435
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:02,120
Do I need help?

436
00:19:02,170 --> 00:19:04,450
Mathieu: And what if the answers aren't clear?

437
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:07,000
Léa: Then, you can give yourself time.

438
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,800
Some important decisions deserve some thought.

439
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:13,500
Mathieu: So sometimes, you have to keep going.

440
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:16,100
Sometimes, you have to slow down.

441
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:19,400
And sometimes, you have to change direction.

442
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:23,700
Léa: Exactly. Perseverance shouldn't become an obligation

443
00:19:23,700 --> 00:19:25,490
to suffer or to stay

444
00:19:25,490 --> 00:19:28,300
in a situation that no longer suits us.

445
00:19:28,300 --> 00:19:30,620
Mathieu: I really like that nuance.

446
00:19:30,670 --> 00:19:32,800
It feels more human to me.

447
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:33,900
Léa: Me too.

448
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:35,310
And I think it's a lovely

449
00:19:35,310 --> 00:19:38,000
way to understand our topic.

450
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:39,750
Before giving up on something

451
00:19:39,750 --> 00:19:41,200
that still matters to us,

452
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:43,100
it can help to look for

453
00:19:43,100 --> 00:19:45,500
what's making the situation difficult.

454
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:46,610
Mathieu: And sometimes,

455
00:19:46,610 --> 00:19:48,800
a small change is enough

456
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:50,700
to let us keep going.

457
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:52,940
Léa: Exactly. Now,

458
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:54,520
shall we go back over a few

459
00:19:54,520 --> 00:19:57,420
French expressions from our conversation?

460
00:19:57,470 --> 00:20:02,030
Mathieu: Great idea! Let's start with "baisser les bras".

461
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:05,600
Léa: "Baisser les bras" means giving up your efforts,

462
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:08,200
usually because you're discouraged.

463
00:20:08,200 --> 00:20:11,900
Mathieu: For example: "Even if this project is hard,

464
00:20:11,900 --> 00:20:14,350
I don't want to give up too quickly."

465
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:17,040
Léa: Second expression: "tenir bon".

466
00:20:17,090 --> 00:20:17,850
Mathieu: "Tenir bon"

467
00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:22,000
means staying determined in the face of difficulty.

468
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
Léa: For example: "This period was complicated,

469
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,120
but she managed to hold on."

470
00:20:27,170 --> 00:20:30,140
Mathieu: Third expression: "se décourager".

471
00:20:30,190 --> 00:20:33,200
Léa: "Se décourager" means losing the drive

472
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:36,900
or the confidence you need to keep going.

473
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:38,010
Mathieu: For example:

474
00:20:38,010 --> 00:20:42,200
"When I don't understand a conversation in French,

475
00:20:42,200 --> 00:20:46,050
I sometimes tend to get discouraged."

476
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:50,720
Léa: Fourth expression: "encaisser un coup dur".

477
00:20:50,770 --> 00:20:54,020
Mathieu: It means going through a big

478
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:58,100
disappointment or a difficult experience.

479
00:20:58,100 --> 00:21:01,700
Léa: For example: "After that bad news,

480
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:05,050
I needed time to take the blow."

481
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:07,210
Mathieu: Fifth expression:

482
00:21:07,260 --> 00:21:10,750
"reprendre là où on s'était arrêté".

483
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:15,500
Léa: It means starting an activity again after a break,

484
00:21:15,500 --> 00:21:19,220
without necessarily going back to the very beginning.

485
00:21:19,270 --> 00:21:20,260
Mathieu: For example:

486
00:21:20,310 --> 00:21:23,800
"I interrupted my studies for a while,

487
00:21:23,800 --> 00:21:25,060
but now,

488
00:21:25,060 --> 00:21:28,450
I'm going to pick up where I left off."

489
00:21:28,500 --> 00:21:33,150
Léa: And our last expression: "avancer à son rythme".

490
00:21:33,200 --> 00:21:36,850
Mathieu: It means making progress while respecting

491
00:21:36,850 --> 00:21:41,200
your own abilities and circumstances.

492
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:42,360
Léa: For example:

493
00:21:42,410 --> 00:21:45,360
"I'd rather go at my own pace

494
00:21:45,360 --> 00:21:48,420
than constantly compare myself to others."

495
00:21:48,470 --> 00:21:50,950
Mathieu: There you go, six expressions you can

496
00:21:50,950 --> 00:21:55,400
easily use in your everyday conversations.

497
00:21:55,400 --> 00:21:56,400
Léa: And now,

498
00:21:56,400 --> 00:21:58,900
let's see how to express some ideas

499
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:01,900
in a more natural way in French.

500
00:22:01,900 --> 00:22:06,650
Mathieu: Let's start with: "I have no motivation left."

501
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:07,600
Léa: You can say:

502
00:22:07,650 --> 00:22:11,650
"Right now, I'm struggling to find motivation."

503
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:14,250
Mathieu: Or: "I'm going through a period where

504
00:22:14,250 --> 00:22:18,020
I have less energy for my projects."

505
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:19,000
Léa: Very good.

506
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:20,580
These sentences show

507
00:22:20,580 --> 00:22:23,800
that the difficulty can be temporary.

508
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:27,750
Mathieu: Second sentence: "I'm lazy."

509
00:22:27,800 --> 00:22:28,630
Léa: You can say:

510
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:29,960
"I'm finding it hard to keep

511
00:22:29,960 --> 00:22:32,650
a regular routine at the moment."

512
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:34,510
Mathieu: Or: "I haven't yet

513
00:22:34,510 --> 00:22:37,650
found a rhythm that suits me."

514
00:22:37,700 --> 00:22:40,950
Léa: Exactly. Third sentence:

515
00:22:40,980 --> 00:22:44,320
"I failed, so I want to stop."

516
00:22:44,370 --> 00:22:45,360
Mathieu: You could say:

517
00:22:45,410 --> 00:22:48,800
"This attempt didn't work out the way I hoped,

518
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:50,950
but I can try a different way."

519
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:52,670
Léa: Or: "I'm disappointed,

520
00:22:52,670 --> 00:22:57,450
but I'd rather step back before deciding."

521
00:22:57,500 --> 00:23:02,950
Mathieu: Fourth sentence: "I'll start again on Monday."

522
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:07,320
Léa: You can say: "I'll get back to it as soon as possible."

523
00:23:07,370 --> 00:23:09,010
Mathieu: Ah, "s'y remettre"!

524
00:23:09,060 --> 00:23:11,430
It means starting again

525
00:23:11,430 --> 00:23:15,050
an activity you'd interrupted.

526
00:23:15,100 --> 00:23:17,610
Léa: Exactly. For example:

527
00:23:17,660 --> 00:23:19,900
"I'd stopped reading regularly,

528
00:23:19,900 --> 00:23:22,150
but I'd like to get back into it."

529
00:23:22,200 --> 00:23:26,850
Mathieu: Fifth sentence: "I have to do everything today."

530
00:23:26,900 --> 00:23:31,050
Léa: You can say: "I'm going to focus on what matters most."

531
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:35,720
Mathieu: Or: "I'll start with what matters most."

532
00:23:35,770 --> 00:23:37,950
Léa: And our last sentence:

533
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,400
"If I can't do everything,

534
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:42,850
I'd rather do nothing."

535
00:23:42,900 --> 00:23:43,840
Mathieu: You can say:

536
00:23:43,890 --> 00:23:47,050
"I'll do what I can today,

537
00:23:47,050 --> 00:23:49,550
even if it's not perfect."

538
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:52,500
Léa: There you go. These expressions let us talk

539
00:23:52,500 --> 00:23:54,740
about our difficulties without being

540
00:23:54,740 --> 00:23:57,800
needlessly hard on ourselves.

541
00:23:57,800 --> 00:23:58,910
Mathieu: You know, Léa,

542
00:23:58,910 --> 00:24:01,990
this conversation made me want to rethink

543
00:24:01,990 --> 00:24:04,920
my famous Sunday-evening plan.

544
00:24:04,970 --> 00:24:07,450
Léa: Oh really? What are you going to change?

545
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:11,620
Mathieu: I'm going to pick two habits instead of six.

546
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:14,090
Walking a bit more often

547
00:24:14,090 --> 00:24:17,300
and reading a few pages before bed.

548
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:20,000
Léa: That sounds much more realistic.

549
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,050
Mathieu: And if I miss a day,

550
00:24:22,050 --> 00:24:26,500
I won't wait for the following Monday to start again.

551
00:24:26,500 --> 00:24:28,450
Léa: Excellent decision!

552
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:32,020
Mathieu: I'll simply pick it up again when I can.

553
00:24:32,070 --> 00:24:35,270
Léa: That's it. You don't need to be perfectly

554
00:24:35,270 --> 00:24:38,800
motivated every day to build a habit.

555
00:24:38,800 --> 00:24:40,360
Mathieu: And I think I'm also going

556
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:44,500
to learn to notice my small wins.

557
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:46,300
Léa: That's a very good idea.

558
00:24:46,300 --> 00:24:48,440
Because when we only look at

559
00:24:48,440 --> 00:24:50,400
what's left to do,

560
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:54,200
we sometimes forget how far we've already come.

561
00:24:54,200 --> 00:24:56,520
Mathieu: Yes. And a bit of progress can

562
00:24:56,520 --> 00:24:59,820
remind us why we started.

563
00:24:59,870 --> 00:25:04,300
Léa: Exactly. So, dear friends, before we finish,

564
00:25:04,300 --> 00:25:07,400
we'd like to ask you a little question.

565
00:25:07,400 --> 00:25:09,200
Mathieu: Is there something

566
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:12,110
in your life you want to keep doing,

567
00:25:12,110 --> 00:25:14,550
even if it isn't always easy?

568
00:25:14,600 --> 00:25:18,920
Léa: Maybe learning French, getting back into an activity,

569
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:22,100
working on a project or simply getting back

570
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:24,500
a habit that's good for you.

571
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:26,050
Mathieu: In the comments,

572
00:25:26,050 --> 00:25:28,220
you can complete this sentence:

573
00:25:28,270 --> 00:25:30,700
"Even if it isn't always easy,

574
00:25:30,700 --> 00:25:33,300
I want to keep..."

575
00:25:33,300 --> 00:25:36,380
Léa: Or: "Right now, I'm struggling to...,

576
00:25:36,380 --> 00:25:39,050
but I don't want to give up."

577
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:42,090
Mathieu: One or two sentences are enough.

578
00:25:42,140 --> 00:25:44,200
Don't be afraid of making mistakes.

579
00:25:44,200 --> 00:25:46,320
It's by using French

580
00:25:46,320 --> 00:25:50,200
that you gradually learn to express yourself better.

581
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:54,000
Léa: Exactly. And if you enjoyed this episode,

582
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,820
remember to leave a little thumbs-up,

583
00:25:56,870 --> 00:26:00,090
subscribe to The Cozy French and share

584
00:26:00,090 --> 00:26:02,730
our conversation with someone

585
00:26:02,730 --> 00:26:04,500
who might enjoy it.

586
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:08,600
Mathieu: Thank you so much for spending this time with us.

587
00:26:08,600 --> 00:26:10,200
Before we go, Léa,

588
00:26:10,200 --> 00:26:13,400
I'd like to ask you one last question.

589
00:26:13,400 --> 00:26:14,800
Léa: I'm listening.

590
00:26:14,800 --> 00:26:17,360
Mathieu: What would you say to someone

591
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:20,950
who feels completely discouraged today?

592
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:25,220
Léa: I'd tell them they don't need to solve everything right away.

593
00:26:25,270 --> 00:26:26,790
They can rest,

594
00:26:26,790 --> 00:26:29,800
think about what still matters to them

595
00:26:29,800 --> 00:26:34,300
and then choose a small step suited to their situation.

596
00:26:34,300 --> 00:26:38,550
Mathieu: And if they still want to pursue their goal?

597
00:26:38,600 --> 00:26:42,100
Léa: Then, they can give themselves a new chance.

598
00:26:42,100 --> 00:26:44,950
Not necessarily by working harder,

599
00:26:44,950 --> 00:26:46,550
but maybe by finding

600
00:26:46,550 --> 00:26:49,020
a different way to keep going.

601
00:26:49,070 --> 00:26:50,500
Mathieu: I love that.

602
00:26:50,550 --> 00:26:54,310
Because you can move forward gently without giving up

603
00:26:54,310 --> 00:26:56,900
what still means something to you.

604
00:26:56,900 --> 00:27:01,070
Léa: Exactly. And a difficult period doesn't mean

605
00:27:01,070 --> 00:27:04,800
all our past efforts were for nothing.

606
00:27:04,800 --> 00:27:07,340
Mathieu: So, don't give up too quickly

607
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:09,800
on what still matters to you.

608
00:27:09,800 --> 00:27:12,400
Léa: But take care of yourselves too,

609
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:15,140
respect your limits and remember

610
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:17,520
that you have the right to slow down.

611
00:27:17,570 --> 00:27:19,100
Mathieu: This was Mathieu...

612
00:27:19,100 --> 00:27:22,000
Léa: And Léa, from The Cozy French!

613
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,190
Mathieu: We'll see you very soon

614
00:27:24,190 --> 00:27:27,300
for a new conversation in French.

615
00:27:27,300 --> 00:27:31,100
Léa: Take care, keep learning at your own pace,

616
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:32,610
and don't forget:

617
00:27:32,610 --> 00:27:35,300
every bit of progress deserves to be recognised.

618
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:37,450
Mathieu: See you very soon, dear friends!

619
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:39,220
Léa: See you soon!
